  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a control-theoretic view of quantum systems and how trusted versus untrusted resources can be combined. One panel shows the abstract geometry: a shaded region of physically allowed dynamics, a line labeled \\(S\\), and a smaller subregion marked as separable, indicating a distinction between stable dynamics and a subset with restricted correlations.\n\nThe remaining panels use block diagrams to compare different experimental settings. A central process \\(W\\) links an input state \\(|\\Psi\\rangle\\) to an output object such as \\(C\\) or \\(M\\), while local operations \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and sometimes \\(C\\) act as inserted modules. Dark blue denotes trusted components, light blue untrusted ones, yellow target operations, and blue dots control settings.\n\nAcross the examples, the same basic process is adapted to different scenarios: fully trusted preparation and measurement around a process matrix, splitting the process into two untrusted parts connected by a Bell pair, or using a Bell pair to feed either the process or one measurement device. Another configuration shows a measurement outcome built from three local operations. Overall, the figure compares architectures for certifying or probing quantum processes under different assumptions about which devices are trusted and how entanglement is used.",
